---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'VIDEO', title: 'One take, three proofs', text: 'In a single uninterrupted take, show the location, that you do the work and that you run the business. Proof of management is the one people forget most.' },
  { label: 'POSTCARD', title: 'Request it once', text: 'Postcards take time to arrive, and delivery times vary by country. If you request a new one while the first is on its way, the first card’s code becomes invalid and the wait gets longer.' },
  { label: 'APPEAL', title: 'Fix the cause first', text: 'Don’t submit a review request for a suspended profile before fixing the cause. In the request, write what you fixed and attach documents.' },
  { label: 'DUPLICATE', title: 'Don’t open a new profile', text: 'A second listing for the same business counts as a duplicate and gets suspended too. In every case, work through the existing profile.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'yontemler', label: 'Verification methods' },
  { id: 'video', label: 'Video rejected' },
  { id: 'kart', label: 'Postcard didn’t arrive' },
  { id: 'askiya', label: 'Profile suspended' },
  { id: 'sahiplik', label: 'Someone else owns the profile' },
  { id: 'beklerken', label: 'What not to do while waiting' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How long does verification take?',
    a: 'Video verification is decided within a few days, sometimes within a few hours. A postcard takes longer, and delivery times vary by country. If a video review has gone past two weeks, write to Google support.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my profile visible while verification is in progress?',
    a: 'No. An unverified profile isn’t published in search or on Maps, however complete it is.',
  },
  {
    q: 'If it’s rejected, can I try again?',
    a: 'You can, once you’ve fixed the reason for the rejection. Failed attempts in a row slow the process down.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Wouldn’t it be faster to open a new profile?',
    a: 'No, that’s the worst move. A second listing for the same business gets flagged as a duplicate and suspended too, and both listings end up in trouble. Work through the existing profile.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="guides/verification"
  title="Google Business Profile Verification Problems | Wiserrise"
  description="Video verification rejected, postcard never arrived, profile suspended or in someone else’s hands: what to do in each case, step by step."
  eyebrow="GUIDE · VERIFICATION"
  h1="How do you fix Google Business Profile verification problems?"
  crumb="Verification problems"
  lede="When verification gets stuck, the fix is to complete the missing requirement on the same profile. If the video was rejected, show the location, the work and the management in a single take; if the postcard didn’t arrive, check the address line and request it again once. Whatever your situation, don’t open a new profile."
  schemaType="article"
  datePublished="2026-08-01"
  dateModified="2026-11-06"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Guides', href: p('guides') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'How do you create a Google Business Profile?', href: p('guides/create-profile'), text: 'The setup before verification and what to do the day the profile goes live.' },
    { label: 'Business not showing on Google', href: p('guides/not-showing'), text: 'If the profile is verified and still not showing, rule out the eight causes one by one.' },
    { label: 'Ranking higher on Google Maps', href: p('guides/rank-higher'), text: 'The order of work for getting a newly live profile ahead of your competitors.' },
    { label: 'Google Business Profile Management', href: p('services/gbp'), text: 'We manage your category, service structure, reviews and posts together.' },
  ]}
  relatedHeading="Related pages"
  ctaLabel="Audit your profile for free"
  ctaHref={p('tools/gbp-audit')}
>
  <h2 id="yontemler">Which verification method will you get?</h2>

  <p>
    Google chooses the method: it looks at the type of business, its category and your account’s history. You may be
    offered video, a postcard, phone, email or a live video call.
  </p>

  <p>
    In sensitive fields like healthcare, law and finance, Google offers stricter methods and reviews the profile by hand.
    That’s normal; the process just takes longer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="video">Why does video verification get rejected?</h2>

  <p>
    A video gets rejected when one of the proofs is missing or the recording doesn’t follow the rules. This is the method
    where people get stuck most. It must be a single uninterrupted take and show these three proofs in order:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Location.</strong> Start outside: the street, the building, the door number and the sign. For a business
      without a sign, this is the weakest link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Operations.</strong> Go inside and show that you really do the work: equipment, workspace, stock, team.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Management.</strong> Show that the business belongs to you: access to the cash register, your business
      license or registration, a utility bill, keys or business documents.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>Reasons videos get rejected, starting with the most common:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>The sign or door number never appears</li>
    <li>The address doesn’t match the pin on the map, meaning you forgot to drag the pin to the right place by hand</li>
    <li>The recording is cut or uploaded in more than one part</li>
    <li>The proof of management is skipped</li>
    <li>Recording at a home address and making it look as if you receive customers there</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Rehearse before you record.</strong> Every failed attempt drags the process out. Note what you’ll show and in
      what order, do one rehearsal, and only then move on to the real recording.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="kart">What should you do if the postcard doesn’t arrive?</h2>

  <p>
    Check your address line first, then request a new one only once. Delivery times vary by country, so wait out the
    period Google shows you.
  </p>

  <p>
    Say your accounting office is on the third floor of an office building in Adelaide. If the address lacks the suite
    and floor number, if the building has no mailbox, or if reception or a neighbor took the postcard, it may never reach
    you.
  </p>

  <p>
    You can request a new postcard from the dashboard, but <strong>don’t request a second one while the first is on its
    way</strong>. A new request invalidates the first card’s code, and the wait gets longer. Request once and wait out the
    full period.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the second postcard doesn’t arrive either, ask Google support for a different method. Look at your address format
    again too: when parts like the suite, unit or floor number don’t fit Google’s form and get left out, the postcard
    can’t be delivered.
  </p>

  <h2 id="askiya">What should you do if your profile is suspended?</h2>

  <p>
    Find and fix the cause first, then submit a review request. A suspended profile disappears from search entirely, and
    the dashboard says “Suspended”. Most owners notice late, because they never see a notification.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Find the cause and fix it.</strong> The most common causes are keywords added to the business name, a virtual
      office address, a name, address and category changed several times in a short period, and more than one profile at
      the same address. Appealing without fixing only drags the process out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Submit a review request.</strong> Write clearly what you fixed and attach the documents you have: business
      license or registration, lease agreement, a utility bill, a photo of the sign.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wait.</strong> The review takes anywhere from days to weeks. Don’t make any other changes to the profile
      during that time.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    <strong>Don’t open a new profile.</strong> It’s the first move everyone thinks of when they’re stuck, and it makes
    things worse every time. A second listing for the same business counts as a duplicate and gets suspended too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sahiplik">How do you get a profile back from someone else?</h2>

  <p>
    With an ownership request. Find the profile on Google and click the “Own this business?” link. An agency, a former
    employee or the person you took the business over from may have created the profile earlier.
  </p>

  <p>
    When you submit the request, the current owner is notified and given time to respond. If they don’t respond, the way
    to claim the profile opens up for you. If they do, a dispute process starts and you may be asked for documents.
  </p>

  <p>
    This process can take weeks. If you have a business license or a company registration record, attach it from the start; it
    speeds things up. Don’t try to get around the problem by opening a new profile, because that doesn’t work here either.
  </p>

  <h2 id="beklerken">What shouldn’t you do while you wait?</h2>

  <p>Don’t touch the profile while verification or a review is in progress. These four moves drag things out in every case:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Changing the name, address or category.</strong> A change made during a review resets the process.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Opening a new profile.</strong> It counts as a duplicate, and you end up with two problem listings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Starting to collect reviews.</strong> Reviews on a profile that isn’t live don’t show, and arriving as a
      sudden pile, they can get caught by the filter.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Paying people who sell “guaranteed verification”.</strong> These offers rely on fake documents or fake
      addresses. When they’re caught, the profile can be closed permanently.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    We list what to do once verification is done in the
    <a href={`${p('guides/create-profile')}#ilk-gun`}>day-one section of the profile creation guide</a>.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Verification is done, so <span class="highlight">what’s next</span>?</h2>
    <p>
      The free audit tool shows what your profile is missing in 10 questions. For every item that comes out weak, what to
      do appears on screen right away, along with the result.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
